          "confidence": 0.96,
          "synopsis": "Although the one sample 𝑡-test has its uses, it’s not the most typical example of a 𝑡-test7. A much more common situation arises when you’ve got two different groups of observations. In psychology, this tends to correspond to two different groups of participants, where each group corresponds to a different condition in your study. For each person in the study, you measure some outcome variable of interest, and the research question that you’re asking is whether or not the two groups have the same population mean. This is the situation that the independent samples 𝑡-test is designed for.",
