          "confidence": 0.92,
          "synopsis": "A worked case study comparing two versions of a course exam. After verifying independence and distribution conditions, the standard error is computed as SE = 4.62, the test statistic as T = 1.15, and degrees of freedom as 45.97 (or 26 using the smaller of n1 − 1 and n2 − 1). The t-distribution with 26 df and the p-value are shown as shaded areas.",
          "pages": [
            307,
            308
          ],
          "figure_ids": [
            "fig_f213361bf057b0_4360_v2"
          ],
          "chunk_ids": [
            "chk_f213361bf057b0_819_v2"
          ],
          "hit_chunk_id": "chk_f213361bf057b0_819_v2",
          "hit_text": "After verifying the conditions for each sample and conﬁrming the samples are independent of each other, we are ready to conduct the test using the t-distribution. In this case, we are estimating the true diﬀerence in average test scores using the sample data, so the point estimate is x¯A−x¯B = 5.3. The standard error of the estimate can be calculated as\n\nSE =\n\ns2A nA\n\ns2B nB\n\n+\n\n=\n\n142 30\n\n202 27\n\n+\n\n= 4.62\n\nFinally, we construct the test statistic:\n\npoint estimate − null value SE\n\nT =\n\n=\n\n(79.4 − 74.1) − 0 4.62\n\n= 1.15\n\nIf we have a computer handy, we can identify the degrees of freedom as 45.97. Otherwise we use the smaller of n1 − 1 and n2 − 1: df = 26.\n\n17H0: the exams are equally diﬃcult, on average. µA − µB = 0. HA: one exam was more diﬃcult than the other, on average. µA − µB = 0.\n18(a) Since the exams were shuﬄed, the “treatment” in this case was randomly assigned, so independence within and between groups is satisﬁed. (b) The summary statistics suggest the data are roughly symmetric about the mean, and the min/max values don’t suggest any outliers of concern.\n\nT = 1.15\n\n−3 −2 −1 0 1 2 3\n\nFigure 7.17: The t-distribution with 26 degrees of freedom and the p-value from exam example represented as the shaded areas.",
